  #text(size: 10pt)[
    - *Q1–3.* Agriculture = deliberate cultivation of crops + rearing of animals (text 1.1). Shifting cultivation abandons land after a few years; settled agriculture keeps the same plot continuously.
    - *Q7–8.* Shifting cultivation cleared land for only one or two seasons; hunter-gatherers still live in desert areas, the Arctic region, and pockets of tropical rain forest.
    - *Q18–19.* Vavilov's concept of centers of origin; Ethiopia is a center for teff, coffee, enset and sorghum (standard knowledge, complementing the text).
    - *Q27.* Industrialized-region eras: Mechanical (1930–1950), Chemical (1950–1970), Biotechnology/IT (1970–present).
    - *Q28–31.* Ethiopia has about 51 million ha of arable land, only ~20% cultivated; the five major crops (teff, wheat, maize, sorghum, barley) cover ~75% of cultivated area; these grains supply ~64% of calories.
    - *Q38–39.* Monocots store food in the endosperm (starch); dicots like legumes and oil seeds store food in the cotyledons (protein, oil, carbohydrates).
    - *Q45–46.* C3 crops: the first stable compound has 3 carbon atoms (wheat, teff, soybean); C4 crops accumulate CO2 in the chloroplast and give high photosynthetic rates at low CO2 (sorghum, maize, sugar cane).
    - *Q48–56.* Mono cropping = one crop per plot (efficient but risk of total failure); mixed cropping = two or more crops at once; intercropping = more than one crop in regular rows at the same time; relay planting = second crop planted after the first has grown alone; rotation improves soil fertility especially with legumes.
    - *Q59–60.* The Konso people practice traditional terracing and irrigation; highland farmers use kitchen ash to fertilize the soil and reduce acidity, and smoke to reduce frost damage.
